    text: "The estate agency sits on the high street between a solicitor's office and a coffee shop — a prime location for catching the eye of passing house-hunters. The window display features colour photographs of properties for sale, each with key details: price, number of bedrooms, and location. Inside, desks are arranged in an open-plan office where negotiators take calls, arrange viewings, and field enquiries from anxious buyers and impatient sellers. The branch manager oversees the team and handles valuations — visiting homes to assess their market value based on size, condition, location, and comparable recent sales. When a property is listed, the agency creates particulars: a detailed description with floor plans, photographs, and an Energy Performance Certificate (EPC) rating. Viewings are arranged — sometimes accompanied by the agent, sometimes unaccompanied with a key safe code. Offers come in, and the negotiator's skill lies in managing expectations on both sides. Once a sale is agreed, the process moves to solicitors for conveyancing — the legal transfer of ownership. The entire process from listing to completion typically takes three to six months in England and Wales. Estate agents earn commission — usually 1–2% of the sale price — paid by the seller upon completion.",
